    "id": "tinned-food-and-long-life-milk-boost-ret-7e7083b5",
    "title": "Tinned food and long-life milk boost retail sales after 'Be Ready' campaign launch",
    "titleJa": "Tinned food and long-life milk boost retail sales after 'Be Ready' campaign launch",
    "summaryJa": "People stocking up on long-life food drove retail sales higher October, according to official data.",
    "bodyOriginal": "Tinned food and long-life milk boost retail sales after 'Be Ready' campaign launch\n- Published\nRetail sales rose in October, driven by people buying long-life food such as tinned goods and UHT milk, according to official figures.\nSales volumes were 0.7% higher as people responded to the government's emergency preparedness \"Be Ready\" campaign, the Office for National Statistics (ONS) said.\nThe campaign encouraged people to stock up in case of disruption to essential services by severe weather or cyberattacks.\nSeparate research released last week suggested that while households were stockpiling essentials, they were not panic buying.\nThe government campaign encouraged people to keep three days' worth of food and water in their homes.\nWet wipes, a torch and cash were also recommended.\nData from retail research firm Worldpanel by Numerator indicated last week that people were stocking up on items including tinned meat, bottled water and hand sanitiser.\nThe latest official sales figures, external from the ONS add to the evidence that the public has begun stockpiling.\n\"Supermarkets have told us that sales of long-life food items such as tinned food and UHT milk were boosted by the government's 'Be Ready' campaign,\" ONS chief economist Grant Fitzner said.\n\"Meanwhile fuel sales have been recovering from earlier low levels, despite pump prices rising again.\"\nThe rise in retail sales last month beat some economists' expectations - they had predicted a slight fall - and the increase came after a 0.6% decline in September.\nAs well as sales being boosted by people buying essentials, visits to art galleries and clothing purchases were also higher in October.\nThe boost in sales comes at the beginning of the festive period, which is normally the busiest time of the year for retailers.\nJacqui Baker, partner and head of retail at auditing firm RSM UK, said that \"retailers will want to see out the year with some Christmas cheer before consumers batten down the hatches in the New Year as energy costs skyrocket and inflation ramps up further\".\nTypical energy bills are forecast to go up by £443 per year from January, pushing up the pace of price rises.\nSales of tinned food and bottled water jump after 'Be Ready' campaign launch\n- Published10 November\nWhy should I stockpile and what will I need?\n- Published23 October\nSeparate economic data released on Friday showed the government borrowed slightly less than economists had expected in October.\nBorrowing - the difference between public sector spending and income - was £17bn last month, the ONS said., external\nAlthough that was £0.8bn higher than October last year, it was below analysts' forecasts of about £18bn.\nInterest repayments were £11.2bn - the highest October debt interest figure on record - and £2.8bn more than a year ago.\nThese repayments have been pushed up by higher inflation, as payments on index-linked government bonds are linked to the inflation rate.\nInflation rose to 3.6% in October, mainly because of higher petrol and diesel prices. The rate has been rising in recent months as the Iran war has driven up the cost of energy and fuel.\nGet in touch\nDo you have any views, comments or questions about this story?",
    "bodyJa": "Tinned food and long-life milk boost retail sales after 'Be Ready' campaign launch\n- Published\nRetail sales rose in October, driven by people buying long-life food such as tinned goods and UHT milk, according to official figures.\nSales volumes were 0.7% higher as people responded to the government's emergency preparedness \"Be Ready\" campaign, the Office for National Statistics (ONS) said.\nThe campaign encouraged people to stock up in case of disruption to essential services by severe weather or cyberattacks.\nSeparate research released last week suggested that while households were stockpiling essentials, they were not panic buying.\nThe government campaign encouraged people to keep three days' worth of food and water in their homes.\nWet wipes, a torch and cash were also recommended.\nData from retail research firm Worldpanel by Numerator indicated last week that people were stocking up on items including tinned meat, bottled water and hand sanitiser.\nThe latest official sales figures, external from the ONS add to the evidence that the public has begun stockpiling.\n\"Supermarkets have told us that sales of long-life food items such as tinned food and UHT milk were boosted by the government's 'Be Ready' campaign,\" ONS chief economist Grant Fitzner said.\n\"Meanwhile fuel sales have been recovering from earlier low levels, despite pump prices rising again.\"\nThe rise in retail sales last month beat some economists' expectations - they had predicted a slight fall - and the increase came after a 0.6% decline in September.\nAs well as sales being boosted by people buying essentials, visits to art galleries and clothing purchases were also higher in October.\nThe boost in sales comes at the beginning of the festive period, which is normally the busiest time of the year for retailers.\nJacqui Baker, partner and head of retail at auditing firm RSM UK, said that \"retailers will want to see out the year with some Christmas cheer before consumers batten down the hatches in the New Year as energy costs skyrocket and inflation ramps up further\".\nTypical energy bills are forecast to go up by £443 per year from January, pushing up the pace of price rises.\nSales of tinned food and bottled water jump after 'Be Ready' campaign launch\n- Published10 November\nWhy should I stockpile and what will I need?\n- Published23 October\nSeparate economic data released on Friday showed the government borrowed slightly less than economists had expected in October.\nBorrowing - the difference between public sector spending and income - was £17bn last month, the ONS said., external\nAlthough that was £0.8bn higher than October last year, it was below analysts' forecasts of about £18bn.\nInterest repayments were £11.2bn - the highest October debt interest figure on record - and £2.8bn more than a year ago.\nThese repayments have been pushed up by higher inflation, as payments on index-linked government bonds are linked to the inflation rate.\nInflation rose to 3.6% in October, mainly because of higher petrol and diesel prices. The rate has been rising in recent months as the Iran war has driven up the cost of energy and fuel.\nGet in touch\nDo you have any views, comments or questions about this story?",
    "source": "BBC",
