    "id": "investigators-make-progress-in-hunt-for-1b47eb2b",
    "title": "Investigators make progress in hunt for cause of FAA ground stop",
    "titleJa": "Investigators make progress in hunt for cause of FAA ground stop",
    "summaryJa": "Engineers and analysts are still searching for the root cause of the issue, which led to a four-hour ground stop on Friday.",
    "bodyOriginal": "Engineers and analysts searching for the root cause of the technical issues behind Friday's nationwide ground stop by the Federal Aviation Administration have been able to replicate the situation that was overwhelming parts of the air traffic system, people familiar with the investigation told CNBC.\nWhile replicating the issue in a lab in Florida is progress, those investigating the matter have yet to determine exactly why two routers in question failed, according to the people, who requested anonymity to talk about an ongoing situation. Finding the root cause is critical to the FAA determining if there may be an issue with other routers that form the backbone of communications for the national air transportation network.\nThe ground stop lasted for more than four hours Friday, marking the longest halt to U.S. flight departures since 9/11.\nTransportation Secretary Sean Duffy and FAA Administrator Bryan Bedford have said there is no indication the routers failed due to a cyberattack.\nThe incident in question happened shortly after L3Harris Technologies, the primary contractor supporting the FAA telecommunications network, installed a new router at a facility in El Dorado, Arkansas.\nL3Harris said that it, in partnership with other tech companies, has completed more than 70% of the planned airspace communications conversion from legacy copper wires to a fiber-optic system.\n\"We are supporting the FAA in its investigation to understand the conditions that led to the Nov. 13 ground stop of our nation's air travel,\" the statement said. \"L3Harris takes the nationwide ground stop and its impact on travelers, airlines and air traffic operations seriously.\"\nInvestigators said within eight minutes of its installation, the router began to transmit too much data within the FAA network.\nThat surge of data, 1,000 connection attempts every four to five seconds, destabilized aircraft communications and radar feeds, Duffy said.\nSince the ground stop, Duffy has increased the amount of money he wants to upgrade the national air traffic system. After originally asking for $30 billion to build a digital network that will incorporate artificial intelligence, Duffy has now asked Congress for $50 billion.",
    "bodyJa": "Engineers and analysts searching for the root cause of the technical issues behind Friday's nationwide ground stop by the Federal Aviation Administration have been able to replicate the situation that was overwhelming parts of the air traffic system, people familiar with the investigation told CNBC.\nWhile replicating the issue in a lab in Florida is progress, those investigating the matter have yet to determine exactly why two routers in question failed, according to the people, who requested anonymity to talk about an ongoing situation. Finding the root cause is critical to the FAA determining if there may be an issue with other routers that form the backbone of communications for the national air transportation network.\nThe ground stop lasted for more than four hours Friday, marking the longest halt to U.S. flight departures since 9/11.\nTransportation Secretary Sean Duffy and FAA Administrator Bryan Bedford have said there is no indication the routers failed due to a cyberattack.\nThe incident in question happened shortly after L3Harris Technologies, the primary contractor supporting the FAA telecommunications network, installed a new router at a facility in El Dorado, Arkansas.\nL3Harris said that it, in partnership with other tech companies, has completed more than 70% of the planned airspace communications conversion from legacy copper wires to a fiber-optic system.\n\"We are supporting the FAA in its investigation to understand the conditions that led to the Nov. 13 ground stop of our nation's air travel,\" the statement said. \"L3Harris takes the nationwide ground stop and its impact on travelers, airlines and air traffic operations seriously.\"\nInvestigators said within eight minutes of its installation, the router began to transmit too much data within the FAA network.\nThat surge of data, 1,000 connection attempts every four to five seconds, destabilized aircraft communications and radar feeds, Duffy said.\nSince the ground stop, Duffy has increased the amount of money he wants to upgrade the national air traffic system. After originally asking for $30 billion to build a digital network that will incorporate artificial intelligence, Duffy has now asked Congress for $50 billion.",
    "source": "CNBC",
